# Public keys allowed to SSH into this machine. Symlinked to
# ~/.ssh/authorized_keys by ssh/install.sh.
#
# I track this file on purpose. Public keys are not secrets -- a public key is a
# lock, not a key -- and keeping mine here is what lets a fresh machine be
# reachable from my other machines right after install.
#
# If you're not me: every key below would get a login on your machine.
# ssh/install.sh prints them and asks before installing, so read that list.
# Clear this file and add your own keys instead.
#
# To authorize a machine, run this ON THAT MACHINE:
#     cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub        # generate first if it has none:
#                                      #   ssh-keygen -t ed25519
# then append the single line it prints here and re-run ssh/install.sh.
#
# Give every key a trailing comment naming its machine -- that comment is what
# install.sh shows at the prompt, and an unlabelled key is one I can't audit.
#
# Format (one per line):
#   ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAA... me@my-laptop

